Description: Screen sleeps after 5 idle minutes; any touch or controller input wakes it
 The XDJ400's touch screen used to stay lit all night. Mixxx now switches
 the display off after [Pioneered] ScreenSleepMinutes (mixxx.cfg, default 5,
 0 = never) with no touch, no key, no controller input and no deck playing.
 A playing deck counts as activity, so the screen never goes dark mid-set.
 .
 New class mixxx::ScreenSleep (src/util/screensleep.{h,cpp}), owned by
 MixxxMainWindow. It is an application-wide event filter (touch, mouse,
 keys) plus a 250 ms poll of [ChannelN],play and of a controller-activity
 timestamp. Controller::triggerActivity(), which runs on the controller
 thread for every incoming message, stores that timestamp in a relaxed
 atomic; nothing crosses threads but the integer.
 .
 Waking: X switches the display back on by itself at any input, and
 controller input wakes it through the poll. The touch that wakes a dark
 screen is swallowed (press through release, plus a 400 ms grace for
 synthesized mouse events, capped at 5 s), so tapping a dark screen never
 presses the button under the finger.
 .
 Display control goes through detached "/bin/sh -c xset ..." processes so
 the GUI thread never waits. DPMS (a real panel power-down) is used when
 the X server has the extension; Raspberry Pi OS disables it whenever
 raspi-config's screen blanking is off, so pi/update-pioneered.sh rewrites
 /etc/X11/xorg.conf.d/10-blanking.conf to load DPMS with every X timer at 0.
 Without DPMS it falls back to the X screen saver's blanking (black, but
 the backlight stays on). At startup X's own blanking and DPMS timers are
 zeroed, so only Mixxx decides when the screen sleeps. The DDJ-400 sends no
 MIDI while untouched (checked with aseqdump), so an idle controller does
 not hold the screen on.
